<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <color name="colorPrimary">#7260CB</color>
    <!-- What Android paints the status bar with, through MAUI's theme. Not a darker shade of the
         primary, despite the Material name: the bar sits directly above Orbit's own top bar, which the
         redesign draws on the page's ground rather than on a lifted surface - so a band of anything
         else above it reads as a second bar. Matches PageBackgroundLight in
         Resources/Styles/Colors.xaml; see values-night for the other half. Only visible up to API 34 -
         from 35 Android draws the app behind the bar instead, and MainActivity paints it below that by
         asking Colors.xaml directly. This file is the one copy that cannot ask: a theme colour is
         resolved when the activity inflates, before there is an Application to read. -->
    <color name="colorPrimaryDark">#F3F2F2</color>
    <!-- What Android tints its own controls with: the line under a focused text field, a checkbox tick,
         a text selection handle. Orbit's default accent, not the MAUI template's indigo, which was a
         colour the app used nowhere on the one mark that says "you are typing here".

         It cannot follow a reader who picks a different accent: a theme colour is fixed when the
         activity inflates, and the obvious lever for changing it afterwards - tinting the widget -
         paints the field's whole background, because MAUI draws that from the same drawable. Following
         the choice properly means giving inputs a drawable of their own. See App.ApplyAccent for the
         colours everything Orbit draws itself does follow. -->
    <color name="colorAccent">#7260CB</color>
    <!-- The home screen widget, which cannot follow the theme chosen inside Orbit: a widget is drawn by
         the launcher in its own process, long after the app has been put down, and all it knows is the
         system's night mode. These are Orbit's own surface and text colours, so it matches the app for
         the reader whose phone and app agree - see values-night for the other half, and
         OrbitTodayWidget for what is drawn with them. -->
    <color name="orbitWidgetBackground">#F3F2F2</color>
    <color name="orbitWidgetText">#201F1D</color>
    <!-- The ink at 45%, flattened against the background above: a launcher may draw the widget over
         wallpaper, so the one place in Orbit that cannot use an alpha ink is this one. -->
    <color name="orbitWidgetSubtleText">#949392</color>
</resources>
